BEGIN CHAPTER 0

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH
"The most difficult, yet basic problem that confronts all chess players is 
coming up with the right plan for a given position."
OK

"At the beginner level, the concept of a plan simply doesn't exist."
OK

"The beginner casts his eye about the chess board, looking lovingly at his 
well-manicured Knights, his pointed Bishop and all powerful Queen."
OK

"With a sudden gleam he thrusts a pawn forward! No! Ooops! Wrong pawn. He 
tries another. Phew! That one was legal."
OK

"When it's his turn to move again, disregarding all threats to his position, 
he proudly moves his Rook. Such daring! Such wit!"
OK

"His opponent deftly captures another piece of the beginner's army." 
OK

"This denuding goes on until the beginner is mercilessly savaged, at which 
point the players set up the pieces and the whole process is repeated."
OK

"If the above sounds like you, don't grieve. This tutorial was made for you!"
OK

"The first way you can improve your game is by learning to look at the whole 
board. Now look at the starting position:"
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
"Imagine yourself on an ancient battlefield with equally matched armies ready 
to fight. With differently skilled weaponry, it is important to see the whole 
battle as it takes place."
OK

"With common foot soldiers, cannon, cavalry and archers, each movement has to 
be coordinated."
OK

"A supply line may become vulnerable and a would-be hero is vanquished."
OK

"Trading an archer for a few foot soldiers may seem sensible, but what if the 
opposing army's archer picks off a number of your foot soldiers from a 
distance without loss?"
OK

"Perhaps the decision wasn't as good as you thought."
OK

"From the starting position, it is clear that battles can begin at once all 
over the board."
OK

"Just as our ancient battle analogy suggests, it is very useful to know 
the terrain of the battle. All the dips and curves of the land. In the 
following diagram"
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

"we have the eight-by-eight chessboard. The chessboard is divided in a 
variety of ways which helps us understand the terrain."
OK

"The board is divided into eight ranks and eight files. The bottom first 
row of squares is the first rank. The second row of squares is the second 
rank and so on."
NEWHIGH a1b1c1d1e1f1g1h1
OK

"From the lower left corner square to the top of the board is a file.  
Starting from the left and working our way over to the right of the board, 
the files are listed as the a-file, b-file, etc."
NEWHIGH a1a2a3a4a5a6a7a8
OK

"By breaking up the chess board into ranks and files, each square is given 
its own coordinates. The lower left corner square is therefore referred to 
as the a1-square."
NEWHIGH a1
OK

"Next to it, the b1-square."
NEWHIGH b1
OK

"The upper left corner square is the a8-square."
NEWHIGH a8
OK

"The upper right corner square is the h8-square."
NEWHIGH h8
OK

"As a good exercise, identify all the highlighted squares in this diagram."
NEWHIGH c6d4g7h2
OK

"By drawing imaginary lines around the board you can divvy-up the board into 
all types of quadrants and sectors."
CLRHIGH
OK

"Drawing an imaginary line between the fourth and fifth ranks neatly divides 
the chessboard in half, with thirty-two squares for each side."
NEWHIGH a1a2a3a4b1b2b3b4c1c2c3c4d1d2d3d4e1e2e3e4f1f2f3f4g1g2g3g4h1h2h3h4
OK

"These thirty-two squares each belong to the White and Black army. White has 
his space, Black has his space."
OK

"When one player controls or attacks one of his opponent's squares this is 
called gaining space."
OK

"It stands to reason that, as we control more and more squares of the board, 
our opponent will have less room to move and become more restricted. The 
effort to control space is a time-honored strategy."
OK

"At the start of the battle the King and Queen are placed next to one another."
CLRHIGH
DIAGRAM "Ke1,Qd1 v Ke8,Qd8" WHITE
OK

"Being the most important pieces, their flanks to the left and right are named 
after them."
OK

"Thus, drawing an imaginary line between the c- and d-files and the f- and 
e-files creates a Kingside and a Queenside.  White's Kingside is the area 
from f1-f4-h4-h1-f1."
NEWHIGH f1f2f3f4g1g2g3g4h1h2h3h4
DIAGRAM "Ke1" BLACK
OK

"Black's Kingside area is a mirror image of White's. Thus, Black's Kingside 
is the area from f8-f5-h5-8-f8."
NEWHIGH f5f6f7f8g5g6g7g8h5h6h7h8
DIAGRAM "Ke8" WHITE
OK

"White's Queenside is the area from c1-c4-a4-a1-c1."
NEWHIGH a1a2a3a4b1b2b3b4c1c2c3c4
DIAGRAM "Qd1" WHITE
OK

"Black's Queenside is the area from c8-c5-a5-a8-c8."
NEWHIGH a5a6a7a8b5b6b7b8c5c6c7c8
DIAGRAM "qd8" WHITE
OK

"Both players tend to castle with their Kings, stowing them to safety on 
either the King- or Queenside. Learning which side is safer will become 
crucial to your King's survival!"
CLRHIGH
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

"The center files are the e- and d-files."
NEWHIGH d1d2d3d4d5d6d7d8e1e2e3e4e5e6e7e8
OK

"Some authors consider these central files to belong to the Kingside or 
Queenside. I do not. It's easier to understand the chess board if you think 
of the e- and d-files as belonging to the center."
OK

"The central files are most often controlled by the Rooks. After the opening 
is over, we are all sagely advised to 'control the center with our Rooks.'"
OK

"The four central squares in the middle of the board, e4,e5,d4,d5 are the 
key central squares. A great amount of the action takes place around them.  
They make marvelous outposts, particularly for the Knights."
NEWHIGH d4d5e4e5
OK

"The player who effectively controls these squares is usually in charge of 
the game."
OK

"Besides ranks, files, flanks and center squares, another way to look at the 
board is along its diagonals. The long diagonals a1-h8 and a8-h1 run right 
through the center of the board."
NEWHIGH a1b2c3d4e5f6g7h8a8b7c6d5e4f3g2h1
OK

"Bishops love to control these diagonals, while being deployed to the Kingside 
or Queenside."
OK

