//=======================================================================
//Chapter Two :  Coordination"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 1
BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

"The first and foremost thing that the aspiring chess player must understand 
is that chess is a game of coordination."
OK

"Chess is a team game. Your pieces and your pawns are your team. In fact, 
they are your army. You are their leader!"
OK

"Once this association is clearly understood,  you will realize the need for 
coordinated actions. If your army is not working as a team, having an overall 
plan of action, you will lose."
OK

"Further, you will not even have a clue as to why you are losing. You will 
continue to be savaged by better-skilled army captains."
OK

"The Principles Have Been Proven"
OK

"Chess is well over 1,400 years old. It has been said that there are more 
books on chess than on any other sport. While this may be hard to prove, the 
point is well-taken. There has been a huge amount of written wisdom on chess."
OK

"A lot of this wisdom can be boiled-down to a few well-honed principles of 
play - principles that you should know and take to heart."
OK

"The most important principles of play are those for the opening. Let's face 
it, if you don't know how to open the game, you're liable to be crushed."
OK

"The following principles don't guarantee safety, but will act as a guide. 
They are:"
OK

"1)  Open the game with a center pawn."
OK

"2)  Develop your Knights before your Bishops."
OK

"3)  Don't move your Queen too soon in the opening."
OK

"4)  Castle early in order to protect your King."
OK

"5)  Develop your Rooks to central or open files."
OK

"6)  Look to develop an attack after you have fully mobilized your pieces."
OK

"7)  Capture towards the center."
OK

"8)  If you violate the above, your opponent will swiftly deal with you!"
OK

"These Opening Principles, taken as a whole, all talk about planning and 
coordination."
OK

"You aim to control the center, mobilize and then, with coordinated motion, 
you go for the thrust and win."
OK

"By using and understanding these opening principles, you will stop being a 
beginner. You may even win a few games."
OK

"You are now ready for your next step on the road to real chess understanding. 
How to plan!  This tutorial will demonstrate how to plan from any type of 
position."
OK

"Planning is based upon the concepts of strategy. Strategy is mobilizing and 
guiding your pieces to their optimal squares to do battle."
OK

"Learning strategy consists of asking oneself simple questions such as, 
'What is my Rook doing there?  Is there a better square for my Rook?'  
Or, 'Uh, oh, my King is feeling the heat. Time to get out of here. Should 
I castle Kingside or Queenside?'"
OK

"Our strategic decisions are based on a number of factors: King safety, 
tactics inherent in the position, the control of space, the pawn structure 
and other considerations."
OK

"This tutorial will focus on the strategic concept of the pawn structure 
and using space."
OK

"What does the term 'pawn structure' mean?  Some chess teachers also use 
the term 'pawn skeleton,' which is more illustrative."
OK

"What both of these terms mean is that, if you strip the position of pieces 
and leave only the pawns on the board, you will clearly see how to plot your 
strategy."
OK

"The pawn structure, or pawn skeleton, reveals lots of things. Firstly, it 
tells us whether a position is open or closed."
OK

"Open positions feature lots of sharp tactics, closed positions feature 
maneuvering games."
OK

"Pawn structures reveal all sorts of other important goodies, too. These 
insights include, open files, open ranks, open diagonals, outposts, locked 
formations, fractured pawns, including pawn islands, isolated pawns, backward 
pawns, split pawns, doubled pawns and dangerous pawns, passed pawns, protected 
passed pawns, as well as pawn chains, base pawns, weak squares and many other 
insights."
OK

"Maybe these terms are unfamiliar to you now, but before too long, you'll know 
them like good neighbors."
OK

"Taking the starting position and removing all the pieces reveals the pawn 
skeleton."
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,d2,e2,f2,g2,h2 v a7,b7,c7,d7,e7,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
OK

"By removing all the pieces from the position, we get the bare bones at the 
start of the game."
OK

"Remembering the ancient battlefield analogy, it stands to reason that if an 
'opening' of the ranks can be forced upon the opponent, then our army could 
flood into that breach and quickly capture the opponent's pieces or, even 
worse, the King! What do I mean?"
OK

"The open file"
OK

"Removing both White and Black d-pawns and we move on to the next example."
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,e2,f2,g2,h2 v a7,b7,c7,e7,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
OK

"This diagram features an open d-file. The d-file is open because there are 
no pawns on it."
OK

"Why are open files important?  Well, can you think of any piece that will 
benefit by sitting on an open file?  If you said Rook or Queen, reward 
yourself with a cookie and send me one too."
OK

"Rooks Love Open Files. This strategic principle only stands to reason. 
Look at the next example."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,e2,f2,g2,h2,Ra1 v a7,b7,c7,e7,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" WHITE
"Both sides have the same seven pawns as before. I've just added a 
Rook to each side."
OK

"If it were White's turn to move, he should do himself an important strategic 
favor and move his a1-Rook to the d1-square as quickly as possible."
OK

MOVE Rd1
"Why?  Because, on the d1-square, White's Rook would be mobilized or developed 
to a position which would allow penetration into the opponent's camp."
OK

"From Black's perspective, he should try to use the same idea, too."
OK

MOVE Rd8
"If he could play ...Ra8-d8, then Black's Rook would be developed on the open 
d-file and he would be primed to shoot down the d-file and invade White's 
position."
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Ke1 v Bg3,Ke8" WHITE
"Challenge:  All else being equal, which recapture by white is better?"
CHALLENGE hxg3 "That's right."
OK

"hxg3."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c3,d4,e3,f2,g2,Ra1,Ke2 v a7,b7,c6,d5,e6,f7,g7,Ra8,Ke7" WHITE
"Challenge:  What is a desirable move for White?"
CHALLENGE Rh1 "Correct!"
OK

"Rh1."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c3,d4,e3,f2,g2,Ra1,Ke2 v a7,b7,c6,d5,e6,f7,g7,Ra8,Ke7" BLACK
"Challenge:  For Black?"
CHALLENGE Rh8 "That is right!"
OK

"Rh8."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,e4,f2,g2,h3,Rd1,Kg1 v a7,b7,c6,e6,f7,g7,h7,Re8,Kg8" WHITE
"Challenge:  Now that White has gained control of the open file,
what next?"
CHALLENGE Rd7 "Excellent!"
OK

"Rd7."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

//=======================================================================
//Chapter Three"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 2

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

"As we have fun altering the pawn skeleton, we have to use different terms to
better understand the transformations that take place. Let's alter the pawn
skeleton a little more to reveal the idea of an open file even better."
OK

"Here is the same diagram with a slight change. The c2-pawn
has moved a file over to become a d2-pawn."
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d2,e2,f2,g2,h2,Ra1 v a7,b7,c7,e7,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" WHITE
OK

"Are there any open files?"
OK

"There are no files that are clear of all pawns. Thus, the answer is no, 
there are no open files."
OK

"From White's perspective, he has a half open c-file. What this means is 
that White has no pawns sitting on the c-file. This makes the c-file open 
to White.  Since Black has a c7-pawn, the c-file is closed to Black."
OK

"The presence of only one pawn means that the c-file is half open. White 
should move his Rook from a1-c1, grabbing his half open file."
OK

MOVE Rc1
"Although the Rook isn't as powerful on a half open file as it is on an 
open file, White has to make due. Not every position has an open file. 
In this case, White's Rook is best placed on the c-file."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d2,e2,f2,g2,h2,Rc1 v a7,b7,c7,e7,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" BLACK
"What about Black?  The same principle applies for him too."
OK

MOVE Rd8
"From his perspective, his half open file is the d-file. By playing 
...Ra8-d8, Black mobilizes his Rook to his best file in this position."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d2,e2,f2,g2,h2,Rc1 v a7,b7,c7,e7,f7,g7,h7,Rd8" WHITE
"Can you tell me why a Rook is more powerful on an open file than on a half 
open file?"
OK

"The answer has to do with the Rook's ability to penetrate. On an open file 
the Rook has the option of penetrating the enemy's camp through all possible 
squares."
OK

"On a half open file the number of possible penetration squares is 
immediately reduced."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Ra1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" WHITE
"Here is a pawn skeleton with Rooks that is very similar to the previous 
diagram. But the pawn structure has changed. What is different?"
OK

"By comparing these two diagrams we can learn some very valuable insights 
into pawn structures. We can also learn how Rooks can outplay one another."
OK

"What are the open files? There are none."
OK

"What are the half open files?  White's half open file is the c-file. Black's 
half open file is the e-file."
OK

"In the previous diagram, Black's half open file was the d-file. What has 
changed?  The e7-pawn has moved to the d5-square."
OK

"The result is that the d-file has become clogged with the d4 and d5-pawns, 
while the e-file has been half opened to Black's Rook."
OK

"Do you notice how White's f2, e3, and d4 pawns are linked together?  
They form a chain. Pawns that are linked together are called pawn chains. 
In this pawn chain, which pawn is the base pawn?"
OK

"The base pawn is the f2-pawn. The base pawn is quite important, since it is 
the foundation upon which the entire pawn chain rests."
OK 

"The strength or weakness of the pawn chain is determined by the base pawn. 
If it is strong, the pawn chain will remain powerful and intact. If weak, 
the entire chain is in danger of collapse."
OK

"Let's now turn to Black's pawn structure. His pawn chain of b5, c6 and 
d5-pawns has an unfortunate disadvantage."
OK

"His base pawn is the c6-pawn, which sits on White's half open file. 
This means that White's Rook will shift over to the half open c-file 
and put a lot of pressure on Black's c6-pawn."
OK

"If the base pawn falls to the moves 1.Rc1 and 2.Rxc6, you can bet that 
the next move will be 3.Rc5. Then Black's pawn chain will be wiped out, 
as the b5 and d5-pawns both lack protection."
OK

"Look again at this diagram. With the limited information we now know, we 
can already perceive that White should start with the move"
OK

"1.Rc1! "
MOVE Rc1
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Rc1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" BLACK
"I reward this move with an exclamation mark to note the excellence of 
the move."
OK

"White understands that, by bringing his Rook to the half open file, he 
firstly develops his Rook and secondly puts pressure on Black's pawn 
structure, forcing him to respond."
OK

"Black, aware that his pawn chain is in immediate danger, is quick to 
respond with"
OK 

"1...Rc8"
MOVE Rc8
OK

"This marks a concession. Both army leaders know that Black's Rook would 
prefer to go to the half open e-file, but, Black has no time!"
OK

"If he were to play 1...Re8 in order to defend his c6-pawn with 2...Re6, 
he would be too late!" 
OK

"After 1.Rc1! Re8? (I place a question mark after this move to denote it as 
a mistake.) 2.Rxc6, White carries out his operation and wins Black's base 
pawn. Soon the rest of Black's pawn chain would fall."
OK

"Black's decision to play ...b7-b5 has weakened his pawn structure."
OK

"Thus, the moves 1.Rc1! and 1...Rc8 have been played. This brings us to our 
next example."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Rc1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f7,g7,h7,Rc8" WHITE
"Here, we can see that Black's c8-Rook is tied down to the defense of his 
c6-pawn. Now why is this?"
OK

"The problem with the c6-pawn is that it can't be protected by another pawn. 
The pawns that flank the c6-pawn, the b5 and d5-pawns have both shot past the 
c6-pawn. The result is that the c6-pawn has been left behind."
OK

"In chess parlance, the c6-pawn is identified as a backward pawn. Backward 
pawns tend to be weak, because they need the protection of other pieces."
OK

"That is, such pawns as backward pawns can't count upon the protection of 
other pawns. As a result, backward pawns are a liability for their owners!"
OK

"Several other points about the this pawn structure should be 
clarified."
OK

"The first thing is that White's pawns are separated into two pawn islands. 
What do I mean?  Well a file, in this case the c-file, separates White's pawns 
into two distinct groups."
OK

"The first group, the a2 and b2-pawns, forms its own pawn island. The second 
group, the d4, e3, f2, g2, h2-pawns, forms the second larger pawn island."
OK

"This concept of different pawn islands is important. The more pawn islands a 
player has, the weaker his position becomes."
OK

"Once again, the reason for this is that pawns - being the weakest team 
members - require protection. If our pawn islands are broken up into smaller 
and smaller groups, their need for protection increases."
OK

"Sometimes our ability to protect them doesn't equal their need!"
OK

"Black also has two pawn islands. The a7,b5,c6 and d5-pawns make one. The f7, 
g7, h7-pawns make up the second."
OK

"In the question of Black's first pawn island, it is important to note that 
Black's a7-pawn, while not touching or in connection with the b5-pawn, is 
still a part of the pawn island."
OK

"If Black didn't have a b-pawn, the a7-pawn would constitute a pawn island 
of its own."
OK

"By looking at the pawn islands of a given position, a principle on how to 
play that particular position becomes clear. The player with more pawn 
islands will have more difficulties in the ending than his opponent will."
OK

"Remember that pawns and pawn islands require protection. As more pieces are 
exchanged, our ability to protect our pawns becomes less and less. Soon the 
player with more pawn islands will have more to worry about."
OK

"From this diagram, how can White further activate his Rook?  His Rook is 
sitting on the half open c-file, doing a good job. Just because a piece is 
performing a task doesn't mean it can't be improved!"
OK

"White realizes that he would like to penetrate his opponent's position with 
his Rook, but, since there are no open files, he has to find another way."
OK

"What about the idea of bringing the Rook up to the third rank and then 
moving across to the a-file?  White could then pressure Black's pawn 
structure from a different direction. How to carry out this operation?"
OK

"The immediate plan of 1.Rc3 intending Rc3-a3 hitting the a7-pawn has a 
drawback."
OK

SLIDE c1 c3 
SLIDE a7 a5 
SLIDE c3 a3 
SLIDE a5 a4
"If 1.Rc3 a5! 2.Ra3 a4! Black's a7-pawn is no longer a weakness."
OK

"Black's a-pawn has now connected itself to the head of the a4, b5, c6-pawn 
chain. In two moves White has given up his pressure on Black's c6-base pawn 
while allowing Black to strengthen his pawn structure."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Rc1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f7,g7,h7,Rc8" WHITE
"Instead of these moves, White tries another tack. He begins with"
OK

"1.b4! "
SLIDE b2 b4
OK

"By playing this move first, White prevents Black from moving his pawn to 
the a4-square. A possible line might go"
OK

"1... f5 2.Rc3 Rc7 3.Ra3"
SLIDE f7 f5
SLIDE c1 c3
SLIDE c8 c7
SLIDE c3 a3
OK

"This maneuver is known as a Rook lift."
OK

"Literally, the Rook is lifted from the back rank up a file in order to move 
across a free rank. White's Rook lift will allow White to mobilize his Rook 
and move it to a more powerful position."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

//=======================================================================
//Chapter Four"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 3

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

DIAGRAM "a2,b4,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Ra3 Kg1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f5,g7,h7,Rc7 Kg8" BLACK
"Black realizes that his Rook doesn't wield nearly the
influence of his opponent's. So he tries to activate his Rook by playing"
OK

"1...Re7"
MOVE Re7
OK

"White realizes that Black has moved his Rook to his
half-open file, where it is ideally placed for further operations.
Not wishing to allow Black even a hint of counterplay,
White tries to tie Black up. White plays Rc3."
MOVE Rc3
OK

"Rc3, attacking Black's c6-pawn, demonstrates
the concept of controlling a half open file."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b4,d4,e3,f2,g2,h2,Ra3 Kg1 v a7,b5,c6,d5,f5,g7,h7,Re7 Kg8" WHITE
MOVE Ra6
"By playing 2.Ra6, White's Rook is now increasing its influence. The
White Rook on a6 attacks both the a7 and c6-pawns."
OK

"In order to protect both pawns, Black is forced to retrace his steps and
play"
OK

"2...Rc7"
MOVE Rc7
OK

"Black's c7-Rook is completely tied down to the defense of
his pawns. If Black runs out of pawn moves and is forced to move his Rook,
he will have to choose which pawn will fall; his c6-base pawn or the a7-pawn."
OK

"This Rook interplay was made possible by the insightful way in which White
approached this position and moved his Rook."
OK

"What made this plan possible?  The pawn structure." 
OK

"Is this plan original? Not at all! Rook lifts occur very often. They don't 
always work; only when the pawn structure or the tactics of a given position 
justifies the idea."
OK

"These first few diagrams reinforce the idea that Rooks would prefer 
positions with lots of open files. Then they would know exactly where 
to go. Precisely! Rooks need open files."
OK

"However, if there are lots of open files, deciding which one to choose 
may not be so easy."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ra1,Rh1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8,Rh8" WHITE
"This is a nice clean position. By now, you should know that both 
sides are going to be locked in a mortal struggle to control the open d-file."
OK

"If you were playing White, you might win with the brilliant strokes 1.Rad1, 
2.Rd7, 3.Rhd1 and so on. Or if you were Black 1...Rad8, 2...Rd2 and 3...Rhd8 
would be equally effective."
OK

"To the victor go the spoils. Thus, the race is on: 1.Rad1 or" 
OK

"1.Rhd1"
SLIDE h1 d1
OK
 
"and now 1...Rad8 or"
OK

"1...Rhd8,"
SLIDE h8 d8
OK

"is quickly played, opposing White's control of the open d-file. Well, 
so much for the race."
OK

"What does White do now?  He would love to force Black to play ...Rd8xd1, 
ceding control over the d-file. But, from the diagram position, this can't 
be done."
OK

"White decides to go for a Rook lift. He plays"
OK

"2.Rd5!"
SLIDE d1 d5
OK

"By attacking both the c5 and e5-pawns, Black is forced to clarify the 
position. The d5-square is an outpost in the position."
OK

"An outpost is a square that can't be attacked by an enemy pawn. The best 
outposts are those squares that we control."
OK

"Here, White's outpost is the d5-square. None of Black's pawns defend the 
square, while two of White's pawns, at c4 and e4, attack the d5-square."
OK

"For the same reasons, Black's outpost is the d4-square. If White had waited 
to play 2.Rd5!, Black would quickly move 2...Rd4!"
OK

"Throughout your chess career, outposts and understanding which squares 
constitute outposts will be a critical part of mastering chess strategy."
OK

"A critical principle to understand is this: As pawns move forward, the 
unprotected squares that they leave behind become potential outposts for 
the enemy army."
OK

"After the move 2.Rd5! we move to the next diagram." 
OK

"Black is faced with a crisis that he must resolve. If the d5-Rook is not 
captured, Black faces the potential loss of either his c5 or e5-pawn."
OK

"Black also faces a more deadly threat. Here, White threatens to play 1.Rad1 
doubling his Rooks on the open d-file."
OK

"The doubling of the Rooks is a key stratagy with Rooks. This doubling of 
power, is also known as creating a battery."
OK

"Batteries are powerful, because they often force an opponent into making a 
pawn weakness or other concessions."
OK

"When a Queen is present, a Rook might work with her to double up and form a 
battery."
OK

"Also, when a Queen is present, she might even triple up on an open file."
OK

"Black cannot allow White's Rook to roost on the d5-outpost. 
Also, he doesn't want to face the possible consequences of"
OK

"1...f6 2.Rad1!"
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ra1,Rd5 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Rd8,Ra8" WHITE
SLIDE a1 d1
OK

"(This move is even stronger than 2.Rxc5 which wins a pawn but gives up 
control of the open d-file.)"
OK

"2...Rxd5 3.Rxd5!"
SLIDE d8 d5
SLIDE d1 d5
OK

"Although White has not yet won a pawn, he is master of the situation. 
Black's Rook is helpless to prevent penetration to the d7-square."
OK

"This conquest of the opponent's seventh and eighth rank is the ultimate 
mission of any Rook. From these ranks, a Rook can perform the maximum damage."
OK

"After 1...Rxd5, White can recapture by 2.cxd5, following the sage principle 
of, 'Capture towards the center.'  In this case 4.exd5 would also be 
reasonable, but, by playing 4.cxd5, White gains a greater influence in the 
center."
OK

"Your reasons for not capturing towards the center should be very specific. 
Keep in mind that, in most cases, you will want to capture towards the center."
OK

"A new position is created."
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d5,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ra1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" WHITE
OK

"Visually, it seems this position is fairly balanced. It is not. White has a 
near-decisive advantage."
OK

"The pawn structure tells us that White has a passed pawn. The d5-pawn is a 
passed pawn because its path to promotion is not blocked by a Black pawn."
OK

"Also, its march up the board cannot be stopped by a Black pawn."
OK

"As chess great Aaron Nimzovitch said, 'A passed pawn is a criminal who 
should be put under lock and key.'  Yes, but who will play guard? A piece. 
Thus an important, powerful member of our team is forced to play guard to a 
lesser player."
OK

"This means that the d5-pawn, a passed pawn, has grown in value. Once he was 
equal to other pawns, but now a piece will have to be assigned to watch him."
OK

"Analysing this pawn structure also gives us an additional secret."
OK

"The d5-pawn is a protected passed pawn. The fact that it is protected by the 
e4-pawn, raises the status of the d5-pawn even more."
OK

"A passed pawn would quickly promote itself if not watched by an opposing 
piece."
OK

"But suppose that White didn't have an e4-pawn. Then the piece that is 
assigned to stop the passed pawn from promotion might simultaneously attack 
the passed pawn, too."
OK

"Just as Black has to tie down a piece to stop the passed pawn from promoting, 
White might have to tie a piece down to the passed pawn's defense!"
OK

"Thus protected passed pawns are mightier than a passed pawn."
OK

"From the position in this diagram, we know that White's half-open file is 
the c-file. Plenty of reason for White to put his Rook there."
OK

"White might play 1.Rc1 and then attempt a Rook lift. He might even try 
1.Rc1 and try the pawn sacrifice 2.b4 in order to blast his way down to 
the seventh rank."
OK

"From Black's perspective, life is bleak. His counterplay is supposed to be 
his half open d-file. But, what's the solace there?  White's d5-pawn is in 
such a dominating position that all of Black's counterplay along the d-file 
is effectively snuffed out."
OK

"This diagram is exactly the same as the previous one with one subtle 
difference. White's c4-pawn has been moved back to the c2-square. This 
small difference of the pawn structure can create a profound change of 
the plan."
DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ra1,Rh1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8,Rh8" WHITE
OK

"We know that White begins 1.Rhd1 and Black responds 1...Rhd8 challenging the 
open d-file. Which Rook lift to make?  Should we try 2.Rd3 or 2.Rd5?"
SLIDE h1 d1
SLIDE h8 d8
OK

"Those of you who tried 2.Rd3 give yourself a slap for failing to understand 
the consequences of your action. For those who thought 2.Rd5! was still best, 
give yourselves a cookie and send me one too."
OK

"2.Rd3? Rxd3 3.cxd3"
SLIDE d1 d3
SLIDE d8 d3
SLIDE c2 d3
OK

"Instead of having a passed pawn, White has created a backward d3-pawn."
OK

SLIDE a8 d8 
SLIDE a1 c1 
SLIDE b7 b6 
SLIDE c1 c3 
SLIDE d8 d4
"Black, being no fool, would immediately place his Rook on his half open 
d-file with 1...Rd8! and after 2.Rc1 b6 3.Rc3 Rd4! it is Black's Rook that 
occupies an important outpost."
OK

"Note that from his outpost, Black's Rook has far more influence than White's."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ra1,Rd1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8,Rd8" WHITE
SLIDE d1 d5 
SLIDE d8 d5 
SLIDE e4 d5"
"In the previous diagram, White should have played 1.Rd5! Rxd5 2.exd5 which 
moves us to"
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,d5,f2,g2,h2,Ra1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ra8" BLACK
"Note the important difference between the two diagrams. White has created a 
passed d5-pawn. But, it is not yet a protected passed pawn. Black to play 
chooses"
OK

"1...c4!"
MOVE c4
OK

"This move cuts the d5-pawn off from protection by the c2-pawn. Black's Rook 
will have to stop the d5-pawn from promotion, but he will simultaneously be 
attacking the d5-pawn. A likely continuation is"
OK

"2.Rd1 Rd8 3.d6"
MOVE Rd1
MOVE Rd8
SLIDE d5 d6
OK

"White has advanced his passed d-pawn a rank, which gives his d1-Rook more 
influence than Black's d8-Rook. Depending upon the other factors in the 
position, Black might be able to save the game."
OK

"The difference between the two diagrams, the pawn being on c2 as opposed to 
the c4-square, considerably changed the plans in the position."
OK

"The final strategical observation to be made here is that Rooks don't like 
blocking passed pawns."
OK

"A Rook that stands in front of a passed pawn is only there because he has 
to be. He would prefer to be wreaking havoc upon the opponent's army, not 
all bottled up as a baby sitter."
OK

"These diagrams show us that all Rooks, while having been created equal, can 
overpower one another based on the pawn structure."
OK

