BEGIN CHAPTER 4

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

"Are all open files the same?  The question itself implies that the answer is
no. And indeed that is the case. Some files are better than others."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,f2,g2,h2 v a7,b7,c7,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
"Here there are two open files. The e- and d-files. Which one
is better for a Rook?  We don't have enough information to tell and the e-
and d-files appear to be of equal value."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c2,f2,g2,h2,Rf1,Kg1 v a7,b7,c7,f7,g7,h7,Rf8,Kg8" WHITE
"If we were to add a pair of Rooks and Kings, we would have a position like
this."
OK

"Now that a few more pieces have been introduced, we can indeed make a
choice. If it was White to move, he should chose 1.Rd1, grabbing the open
d-file. The reason will soon be made clear."
OK

"After 1...Re8, Black counters by grabbing the open e-file."
OK

"White's next move explains his decision on move one. He plays 2.Kf1!,
the purpose of which is to stop Black from penetrating with his Rook."
OK

"Black, however, cannot prevent similar penetration. Black could still play
