OK

MOVE Rd1
"1.Rd1?"
OK

MOVE Rc8
MOVE Kf1
MOVE Kf8
MOVE Rd7
"Then 1... Rc8! 2.Kf1 Kf8 3.Rd7."
OK

"Has White made the same progress as before?"
OK

MOVELIST "Rc1 Ke2 Rc2"
"No! Black now has the clever response 3...Rc1+! 4.Ke2 Rc2+ "
OK

"and, in order to avoid losing a pawn, White has to retreat with 5...Rd2, 
blocking the check."
OK

"These diagrams teach us some important strategical points about open files:"
OK

"1)  Open files are meaningful if the penetration squares aren't protected.  
In the previous diagram, although Black's Rook took the open e-file, White 
neutralized his control by moving his King to the f1-square. Black's Rook 
couldn't penetrate White's camp."
OK

"2)  Given the choice of which open file to take, it is usually better to 
take the one that is farthest from potential defenders."
OK

"In the previous diagram, White grabbed the c-file. This open file was the one 
that was furthest from Black's King, which is a potential defender of the 
open file."
OK

"In my explanation , I called Black's first move, 1...Rd8, a dubious move. Did 
you guess why?  The answer is because Black continued on a course that led to 
disaster."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,e3,f2,g2,h2,Rc1,Kg1 v a7,b7,e6,f7,g7,h7,Rf8,Kg8" BLACK
"Now, it is clear that White is prepared to penetrate to the 
seventh rank. The best way to neutralize this plan is by" 
OK

MOVE f5
"1...f5 "
OK

"in order to prepare ...Rf8-f7. Thus 2.Rc7 Rf7 gets White nowhere.  White 
changes his plan and begins by centralizing his King with"
OK

MOVE Kf1
"2.Kf1"
OK

"Black plays"
OK

