//=======================================================================
//Chapter Six"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 5

CLRHIGH
BOARD 64 WHITE

"This chapter will focus on using pawn skeletons to determine the way to 
develop the Bishop."
OK

"The most important principle about Bishops is that they are long range 
pieces. They hate to be locked into closed positions. Like Rooks, Bishops 
operate best in open positions."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,d5,e4,f2,g2,h2 v a7,b7,c5,d6,e5,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
"Here we see a position from a Bishop's worst nightmare. 
The pawn skeleton shows a position which is totally blockaded. The center 
pawns are locked in place."
OK

"For a Bishop to become effective in such positions, the players will have 
to create a few pawn exchanges."
OK

"Let me show you why closed positions are Bishops worst nightmares.  Let's 
add a Bishop each to both player's positions."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,d5,e4,f2,g2,h2,Bd3 v a7,b7,c5,d6,e5,g7,h7,Be7" WHITE
"This is a classic example of two bad Bishops. We say a Bishop is bad when 
its pawns are on the squares of the same color."
OK

"In this case, White's c4, d5 and e4-pawn completely lock-in White's Bishop. 
It is reduced to a role of passive defender."
OK

"Likewise for Black. His central structure of d6, c5 and e5-pawn neutralize 
his Bishop."
OK

"More than anything, the players of these armies would love to activate their 
Bishops to open diagonals."
OK

"In White's case, he would try to play 1.Be2, 2.Bg4 and 3.Bc8, turning his 
passive white-squared Bishop into a more active role."
OK

"Black shouldn't hesitate either. He should also move his Bishop 'around' the 
blocked center onto an open diagonal by 1...Bg5 and 2...Bc1, activating his 
Bishop."
OK

"As this example shows, for a Bishop to be effective it must have an open 
diagonal!"
OK

"If you have a Bishop that is blocked in by its own pawns, what to do? 
The easiest solution is to exchange it for the opposing Bishop, or perhaps a 
Knight."
OK

"Sometimes this isn't possible. The next try would be to move the pawns out 
of the Bishop's way."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b3,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2,Be2 v a7,b7,c6,e5,f7,g7,h7,Nc8" WHITE
"This diagram features a position of Bishop versus Knight. On the 
surface, it looks like a pretty bleak future for White's Bishop."
OK

"His center and Queenside pawns are all on white squares, the same color 
as the Bishop."
OK

"However, the pawns are not locked. White can 
still mobilize his pawns. He begins by"
OK

MOVE c5
"1.c5!"
OK

"A very good move. Black's Knight is prevented from playing ...Nc8-d6 with 
an attack against the e4-pawn."
OK

"More importantly, the Bishop is given new life: "
OK

MOVE Ne7
MOVE Bc4
"1...Ne7 2.Bc4"
OK

"White's Bishop is now bearing down on the f7-pawn. "
OK

MOVE f6
MOVE Be6
"2...f6 3.Be6"
OK

"In the space of just a few moves, the Bishop has become a powerful piece.  
Should Black's Knight move, then the idea of Be6-c8 attacking the b7-pawn 
will be possible."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b3,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2,Be2 v a7,b7,c6,e5,f7,g7,h7,Nc8" BLACK
"We began with a position that 
looked terrible for White's Bishop. But, as it was his move, we allowed him 
to improve his Bishop at once with the move 1.c5!"
OK

"What if we go back to the example and give Black the first move?"
OK

"If it were Black's move, he would try to ensure that the pawn structure was 
locked up and that White's Bishop was encased. He'd play"
OK

MOVE Nd6
"1...Nd6 "
OK

"attacking the e4-pawn and after "
OK

MOVE f3
MOVE c5
"2.f3 c5! "
OK

"when White would have a truly miserable position."
OK

"Because Bishops are long range pieces that can attack from afar, they are 
beloved by aggressive players."
OK

"They do, however, have some pretty serious drawbacks. The most obvious one 
is the fact that they can only move around to half the squares on the 
chessboard."
OK

"While this drawback may not trouble the Bishop, it can cause you and me a 
lot of agony."
OK

"Imagine yourself as the player in the next diagram."
OK

DIAGRAM "h2,Bd3,Kg1 v Kh8" WHITE
"You have to be pretty pleased with yourself that you've reached such a 
position. You are a Bishop and pawn to the good and you have been expecting 
your opponent to stretch his hand in congratulations."
OK

"Surprisingly, this hasn't happened yet. Slightly annoyed, you now decide 
that it is time to force your extra pawn up the board to promotion and 
checkmate the Black King."
OK

"Much to your befuddlement, this position is a dead draw!"
OK

"Black's defense is to keep his King in the corner, moving it back and forth 
either by ...Kh8-g8-h8 or ...Kh8-g7-h8."
OK

"White can't force Black out of the corner, because the light-squared Bishop 
doesn't control the queening square."
OK

"This rotten piece of luck happens to everyone. With any other pawn the King 
can be forced out the other side and the White King can escort the pawn home."
OK

"But with the a- or h-pawn, the stalemate theme shows up and, in order to 
win, the Bishop must control the promotion square."
OK

"The Bishop can only control half the squares on the chessboard. This fact 
should not be forgotten!"
OK

"Like Rooks, it is possible to create a battery with a Bishop."
OK

DIAGRAM "b4,c5,f2,g3,h2,Be4,Qe2,Kg1 v a7,c6,f7,g6,h7,Bf6,Qc8,Kg8" WHITE
"This example features an open position with a split pawn structure. 
Black has split Queenside pawns. His a7 and c6-pawn are 
disconnected by a file."
OK

"This means that they cannot protect each other, making them vulnerable. 
White has two pawn islands, while Black has three."
OK

"Black's split pawns are vulnerable. In fact, White's Bishop is greedily 
eyeing the little c6-morsel. All White has to do to win the c6-pawn is to 
reinforce the pressure against it."
OK

"White plays Qf3, creating a battery along the h1-a8 diagonal. Coincidentally, 
White's Queen also attacks Black's Bishop."
OK

"The diagram also features a common occurrence known as Bishops of opposite 
colors. Positions with Bishops of opposite colors favor the attacker."
OK

"The reason is the defender won't be able to use his Bishop to block the 
attack. As is the case here. Black cannot defend his c6-pawn with his Bishop."
OK

"While Bishops of opposite colors favor the player with the attack, such 
positions can also make a defender rather happy."
OK

DIAGRAM "e4,d5,Be6,Kd4 v Kd6,Be5" WHITE
"Here White has a sizable material advantage. He is two 
connected passed pawns up."
OK

"But the presence of the Bishops of opposite colors makes the situation a 
dead draw. Black will happily move his Bishop on the long diagonal a1-h8. 
White cannot make further progess."
OK

"Essentially, Bishops of opposite colors allow the players to be the masters 
of the squares on which their Bishops operate."
OK

"The outcome of this game would change if the Bishops 
were of the same colors. Take out a chess board and play it out with the 
Bishop on e6 changed to a Bishop on e3."
OK

"In no time, you should be able to force White's connected passed pawns up 
the board."
OK

"Earlier, we looked at a battery of Queen and Bishop. Such 
batteries come in many varieties. The most often seen are the following two 
examples."
OK

DIAGRAM "a3,b2,e3,f2,g2,h2,Bd3,Qd1,Kg1 v a7,b6,d5,f7,g7,h7,Nc6,Qd8,Kg8" WHITE
"Here, White takes advantage of a tactical double threat to 
play 1.Qc2."
OK

"Attacking the exposed c6-Knight and by making a battery on the b1-h7 
diagonal, Black has to choose whether to lose his Knight or the h7-pawn."
OK

"Before leaving the diagram, two points should be noted. White could also 
have started with 1.Bxh7+ Kxh7 2.Qc2+, which recovers the piece and wins a 
pawn."
OK

"The other point is, did you notice Black's pawn structure?  Black's d5-pawn 
is called an isolated pawn.  This refers to the fact that the pawn cannot be 
protected by another pawn."
OK

"How many pawn islands does Black have?  White?"
OK

DIAGRAM "a3,b2,e3,f2,g2,h2,h3,Bc2,Qd1,Kg1 v a7,b7,d5,f7,g7,h7,Qd7,Kg8" WHITE
"In this example, White creates a battery by putting his Queen in 
front of his Bishop, "
OK

MOVE Qd3
"1.Qd3!"
OK

"White threatens Qd3xh7+ and Black is forced to play either 1...g6 or 1...f5.  
This battery that White has created doesn't win material, but does force Black 
to move a pawn, which creates some weak squares."
OK

"For example, suppose after 1.Qd3, Black chooses "
OK

"1...f5"
MOVE f5
OK

"The result is that Black has weakened the e5-square. White could play"
OK

"2.Qc3 "
MOVE Qc3
OK

"and try to occupy the e5-square, because no Black pawn can protect that 
square."
OK

"What about 1...g6?  In that case, the pawn's move has weakened the f6-square."
OK

"White could use the same move 2.Qc3 and try to occupy the f6-square."
OK

"This combination of understanding the pawn structure with piece play, such 
as creating a battery, is what planning is all about."
OK

"Because a Bishop is a long range piece, it stands to reason that, as the 
game opens up, its value as a member in our army increases. Perhaps in the 
opening and early middlegame, our Bishops didn't always find the right 
diagonals."
OK

"Not so in the ending. As pawns are exchanged, the Bishops have an easier 
time taking part in our plans. This principle of the Bishops reaching their 
potential is best seen in the ending where the position is so open."
OK

"One thing a Bishop can do very well is to dominate a Knight."
OK

DIAGRAM "Bd5 v Nd8" WHITE
"This is a classic example."
OK

"Note how the Black Knight is solidly rooted to its square. Without protection 
it cannot move."
OK

"Let's test the knowledge we have learned by following a game from the big 
leagues."
OK

DIAGRAM "a4,b2,c3,f4,g3,h4,Bf3,Kb5 v a5,b6,c5,f5,g6,h5,Ne7,Kc7" WHITE
"In this game we will see how Bobby Fischer, playing White, methodically uses 
his Bishop to make inroads on Mark Taimanov's position."
OK

MOVE Ka6
"1.Ka6!"
OK

"This move forces Black to move his Knight, because any King or pawn move 
would cost material."
OK

MOVELIST "Ng8 Bd5 Ne7 Bc4"
"1...Ng8 2.Bd5 Ne7 3.Bc4!"
OK

"A subtle move that will soon become clear. White wants to achieve a position 
where it will be Black to move. So, he deliberately makes a waiting move, in 
order to force his opponent to move. This loss of a move is called losing a 
tempo."
OK

DIAGRAM "a4,b2,c3,f4,g3,h4,Bc4,Ka6 v a5,b6,c5,f5,g6,h5,Ne7,Kc7" BLACK
MOVELIST "Kc6 Bf7 Kc7 Be8"
"3...Kc6 4.Bf7 Kc7 5.Be8"
OK

"White has created the maximum position possible."
OK

DIAGRAM "a4,b2,c3,f4,g3,h4,Be8,Ka6 v a5,b6,c5,f5,g6,h5,Ne7,Kc7" BLACK
"Clearly, Black is in trouble. His Knight is chained to the defense of the 
g6-pawn base. If this pawn goes, the whole Kingside structure collapses.  
The King is also tied to the defense of the b6-base pawn."
OK

"Any pawn move costs Black that pawn. Black is forced to play 5...Kd8"
OK

MOVE Kd8
"and Bobby sacrificed his Bishop:"
OK

MOVELIST "Bxg6 Nxg6 Kxb6"
"6.Bxg6! Nxg6 7.Kxb6."
OK

"White has captured both base pawns. Black's compensation, his extra Knight, 
is not enough to stop White's Queenside pawns."
OK

DIAGRAM "a4,b2,c3,f4,g3,h4,Kb6 v a5,c5,f5,h5,Ng6,Kd8" BLACK
MOVELIST "Kd7 Kxc5 Ne7 b4 axb4 cxb4 Nc8 a5 Nd6 b5 Ne4 Kb6 Kc8 Kc6 Kb8 b6" 
"7...Kd7 8.Kxc5 Ne7 9.b4 axb4 10.cxb4 Nc8 11.a5 Nd6 12.b5 Ne4+ 13.Kb6 Kc8 
14.Kc6 Kb8 15.b6" 
OK

"and Black gave up, since he can't prevent White from queening one of his 
pawns."
OK

"These two examples are considered masterpieces of Bishop domination over a 
Knight."
OK

"They are worth playing over and over to see how a Bishop and King work 
together to force through a victory."
OK

"If a lone Bishop in the ending can be so powerful, it stands to reason that 
two Bishops working side by side can do major damage."
OK

"In chess parlance when a player has forced the exchange of a Bishop for one 
of his Knights, he is said to have won the two Bishops."
OK

"Operating either at short or long range, the Bishops can construct a variety 
of mating nets, as the next few examples show."
OK

DIAGRAM "Be8,Bf8,Kc2 v Ka2" WHITE
"This diagram shows a King being driven into a corner by two long range 
Bishops. White begins with"
OK

MOVELIST "Bf7+ Ka1 Bg7"
"1.Bf7+ Ka1 2.Bg7 checkmate."
OK

"In the middlegame, the Bishops can also flex their muscle."
OK

DIAGRAM "Bc3,Bd3,Qb1 v Rf8,f7,g5,h6,Kg8" WHITE
"Black's shielding pawns have moved forward. This allows White to use his 
Bishops to corner Black's King."
OK

"1.Bh7 Checkmate!"
SLIDE d3 h7
OK

"These two checkmating patterns are seen in a variety of ways in many 
grandmaster games."
OK

DIAGRAM "Bc2,Qd1,Ke1 v Na6,Kh8" WHITE
"Challenge:  In this skeletal position, what battery can be formed
with a double attack?"
CHALLENGE Qd3 "That's right."
OK

"Qd3."
OK

BOARD r2q1rk1/2p2ppp/1pn1p3/p2p4/3P4/3BP3/PP3PPP/1R1Q1RK1 WHITE
"Challenge:  White is a pawn down. How should he regain the lost
material?"
CHALLENGE Bxh7 "Correct!"
OK

"Bxh7."
OK

"Challenge:  After the recapture, Kxh7, what is the move?"
MOVE Kh7
CHALLENGE Qc2 "That's right!"
OK

"Qc2."
OK

BOARD r2q1rk1/2p2ppp/1pn1p3/p2p4/3P4/3BP3/PP3PPP/1R1Q1RK1 WHITE
MOVE Qc2
"Challenge:  If White had played the battery-forming move Qd1-c2 first,
how would Black play to avoid losing material?"
CHALLENGE Nb4 "Right."
OK

"Nb4. Then, if White captures, Bd3xh7+ and Black plays the
forced Kg8-h8, would White be able to save the piece?"
OK

"No."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

//=======================================================================
//Chapter Seven"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 6

CLRHIGH
BOARD 64 WHITE

"This Chapter will focus on using the Knight in various pawn skeleton
situations."
OK

"Of all the pieces of our army, the Knight is certainly the most difficult
to master."
OK

"The Knight is the one piece that is able to jump over friendly and unfriendly
forces. Its strange L-shaped movement is difficult to get used to."
OK

"For these reasons and many others, the Knights are unappreciated for all the 
goodness they can bring to a position."
OK

"Here are some practical things to keep in mind. Knights are not long-range 
pieces. To be effective, they have to be close to the action."
OK

DIAGRAM "Na1" WHITE
"Here we have a Knight on a1. How many squares does the he control?"
OK

"Only two. The c2 and b3-squares. Pretty bleak. This is the reason for the 
witticism, 'A Knight on the rim is dim.'"
OK

"Imagine that all the action was on the Kingside, say the g8-square. It would 
cost White five moves to bring his Knight to g8."
OK

"He would try a tour like Na1-b3-d4-f5-e7-g8. By the time the Knight made the 
trip, the action might have shifted to the Queenside and the tour has to take 
place all over again."
OK

DIAGRAM "Ne5" WHITE
"This brings us to a crucial understanding:  To be effective, Knights must be 
centralized."
OK

ADDHIGH c6d7f7g6g4f3d3c4
"Here we see White's Knight attacking eight squares. Brilliant! Whether the 
action is on the Kingside or Queenside, the Knight will have no difficulties 
making himself a part of the scene."
OK

CLRHIGH
"However, this presents a problem. If Knights have to be centralized, it 
means they will have to be protected, else they will be driven off their 
squares."
OK

"This means that Knights must have secured outposts."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
"In this position, White's outposts are the b5, d5 and f5-squares. The reason 
why is that these are the squares that are protected by White's pawns."
OK

"The b5 and f5-squares, however, are not secured. That is, Black could drive 
a Knight away by playing ...a7-a6 or ...g7-g6. The only secured outpost is 
the d5-square."
OK

"From Black's perspective, his is exactly the mirror image of White's line 
of reasoning."
OK

"Now we add a pair of Knights to the position."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,e4,f2,g2,h2,Ng1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Ng8" WHITE
"Here, the race is on for both sides to try to bring their Knights to their 
outposts."
OK

"White starts with "
OK

MOVE Ne2
"1.Ne2!"
OK

"This is superior to 1.Nf3 which attacks a pawn and is quite attractive. The 
only problem is that after 1.Nf3 f6! White's Knight hasn't reached one of his 
outposts."
OK

"If he continues with 2.Nh4, Black's playing g6! keeps White's Knight out of 
the f5-square and he will be forced to rethink his path."
OK

"Black matches White's first move, "
OK

MOVELIST "Ne7 Nc3 Nc6 Nd5 Nd4"
"1...Ne7 2.Nc3 Nc6 3.Nd5 Nd4"
OK

"Both sides have played very well and have moved their Knights to the best 
outposts available."
OK

"In this diagram, White has moved his Knight to the d5-outpost when he 
might have preferred to move it to the f5-outpost, where the Knight could 
attack the g7-pawn."
OK

"The reason he didn't do this is that the f5-outpost was unstable. It couldn't 
be secured. How can an outpost be secured?  The answer is by pawn play."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c4,e4,f3,g2,h4,Na1 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f6,g7,h6,Nh8" WHITE
"In this diagram, both players have a fairly good idea as to where they want 
to move their Knights. The problem for White is that the d5-outpost, as nice 
as it is, doesn't create enough problems for his opponent. He therefore 
starts the race with"
OK

MOVE h5
"1.h5!"
OK

"The purpose of this move is to stop the response ...g7-g6 when the White 
Knight plops down on the f5-square. From the f5-square, the Knight will 
attack the g7-base pawn. Play might continue:"
OK

MOVELIST "Nf7 Nc2 Ng5 Ne3 Ne6 Nf5"
"1... Nf7 2.Nc2 Ng5 3.Ne3 Ne6 4.Nf5"
OK

"White has moved to his outpost before Black could get to the d4-square. As a 
result, Black's Knight is tied to the defense of the g7-pawn."
OK

DIAGRAM "e5,d4, v d5,e6" WHITE
"This example features a locked center pawn struggle."
OK

"The Knight outposts for both sides are straightforward. White would love to 
have a Knight on the c5, d6 or f6-squares. For Black, his choices are the f5, 
e4 or c4 squares."
OK

"Knights love locked pawn formations. In such situations, they know exactly 
what squares to go to."
OK

"What Knights don't like are pawn structures that are in a great deal of 
flux. What was once an ideal landing square may suddenly be taken away."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,c3,d4,e5,f2,g2,h2, v a7,b7,c4,d5,e6,f7,g7,h7" WHITE
"In this locked pawn formation, White's best outpost is the d6-square. While 
the c5 and even f6-squares might be nice, the f6-square is covered by the 
g7-pawn, while the c5-square could be covered by ...b7-b6."
OK

"From Black's point of view, the d3, e4 and f5-squares would all be nice. If 
Black plays ...b7-b5, then bringing a Knight to the a4-square would be 
attractive too."
OK

"But, Black has to be realistic as well. How to bring a Knight to the 
d3-square?  Most likely, it would have to traverse through the f4-square."
OK

"White might prevent this by g2-g3. The e4-square can be blocked by f2-f3. 
That leaves the f5-outpost. This could be covered by g2-g4."
OK

"Therefore in such pawn formations, the move ...h7-h5 is often played in 
order to secure the f5-square."
OK

"If White plays h2-h3 in order to play g2-g4, Black often continues with 
h5-h4 in order to prepare en passant."
OK

"One of the Knight's greatest uses is that of a blockader."
OK

"If your opponent has a protected passed pawn, the Knight does 
terrifically as a blockader. The reason is that the Knight not only blocks 
the pawn, it attacks squares at the same time."
OK

DIAGRAM "a2,b2,d5,e4,f2,g2,h2,Nc3 v a7,b7,c5,e5,f7,g7,h7,Nd6" WHITE
"Here we see that White's protected passed d5-pawn is firmly blocked from 
moving."
OK

"Not only does the d6-Knight stop the d5-pawn,  it simultaneously attacks the 
e4-pawn. This makes the d6-Knight a rather powerful piece that White wants to 
exchange-off."
OK

"To do this, White would have to move his c3-Knight, which might cost his 
e4-pawn. White might plan to play 1.a4 and 2.Nb5. But, fearing the loss of 
his e4-pawn, he starts with"
OK

MOVE f3
"1.f3, securing his pawn chain."
OK

"White has created a new base pawn. His g2-pawn now plays a key role."
OK

"Black might counter White's strategy in two ways, depending upon the needs 
of the position. He might play 1...b6 in order to meet 2.a4 with a6, stopping 
Nc3-b5."
OK

"Or he might try to transfer White's base pawn forward. The way to do this is 
to attack White's pawn chain in the middle. Black might try"
OK

MOVELIST "f5 a4 fxe4 fxe4"
"1...f5 2.a4 fxe4 3.fxe4"
OK

"Now the base pawn is no longer the g2-pawn but is now the e4-pawn. Along 
the pawn chain, base pawns can be moved."
OK

DIAGRAM "Nd3,Ke1 v a6,b7,Ke8" WHITE
"Challenge:  What is White's material
gaining move here?"
CHALLENGE Nc5 "You are correct, sir!"
OK

"Nc5."
OK

DIAGRAM "a3,b4,d4,e3,Nb1,Ke1 v a6,b5,d5,e6,Ke8" WHITE
"Challenge:  What is a desirable Knight maneuver?"
CHALLENGE Nd2 "That's right."
OK

//"Nd2. ..."
SLIDE d2 b3
SLIDE b3 c5
//OK

"Nd2, moving him from b1 to d2, then to b3 and up to c5."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

