BEGIN CHAPTER 7

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

BOARD 2b3k1/7p/p1n5/1pP1pp2/PP2p1p1/2B1P1P1/4NP1P/6K1 WHITE
"Although Knights move around slowly, their advantage over Bishops is that
they do move to both black and white squares. The following example was
taken from my own practice."
OK

"A rather difficult position has arisen. White's pawn skeleton tells us
several things:  1) He has a protected passed pawn. 2) He has a bad Bishop.
3) His Knight has no chance of finding a good outpost."
OK

"Black's pawn structure also reveals several things:"
OK

"1) The doubled e-pawns are usually a grave weakness. While White has a
passed pawn on the Queenside, I have little chance of creating a passed pawn 
on the Kingside."
OK

"2)  Although I have doubled e-pawns, the e5-pawn is magnificent. It denies 
White's Knight access to the d4-square and closes the a1-h8 diagonal."
OK

"3) My c6-Knight perfectly blockades White's passed c5-pawn."
OK

"4)  My c8-Bishop is a bad one as most of my pawns are on light squares."
OK

"These revelations brought about the following plan."
OK

"1) I must activate my Bishop, trying to exchange it off for White's Knight."
OK

"2) I must activate my King, bringing it up the board to keep an eye on 
White's passed pawn while protecting my important e5-pawn. Finally, I can 
then move my Knight around the board to the f3-outpost."
OK

"My opponent, Grandmaster Alex Wojtkiewicz of Poland, now played:  1.Kf1 Be6 
2.Ke1 Bc4 3.Nc1"
MOVELIST "Kf1 Be6 Ke1 Bc4 Nc1"
OK

BOARD 6k1/7p/p1n5/1pP1pp2/PPb1p1p1/2B1P1P1/5P1P/2N1K3 BLACK
"Alex notes that I am trying to exchange-off my Bishop for Knight and tries 
to stop me, for now. His problem, however, is that the c4-Bishop dominates 
the Knight.  3...Kf7 4.Kd1 Ke6 5.Kc2 Nd8"
MOVELIST "Kf7 Kd1 Ke6 Kc2 Nd8"
OK

BOARD 3n4/7p/p3k3/1pP1pp2/PPb1p1p1/2B1P1P1/2K2P1P/2N5 WHITE
MOVELIST "Kd1 Nf7 Ne2 Bxe2+ Kxe2 Kd5"
"Now that the Black King keeps a watchful eye over the c5-pawn, the Knight 
begins his journey to the f3-outpost.  6.Kd1 Nf7 7.Ne2 Bxe2+ 8.Kxe2 Kd5"
OK

DIAGRAM "a5,b4,c5,e3,f2,g3,h2,Bc3,Ke2 v a6,b5,e5,e4,f5,g4,h7,Nf7,Kd5" WHITE
"Black is now ready to bring his Knight to f3."
OK

"9.Kf1"
MOVE Kf1
OK

DIAGRAM "a5,b4,c5,e3,f2,g3,h2,Bc3,Kf1 v a6,b5,e5,e4,f5,g4,h7,Nf7,Kd5" BLACK
"Anticipating ...Nf7-g5-f3, White prepares to keep his h2-pawn protected."
OK

"9...Kc4 10.Be1"
MOVELIST "Kc4 Be1"
OK

DIAGRAM "a5,b4,c5,e3,f2,g3,h2,Be1,Kf1 v a6,b5,e5,e4,f5,g4,h7,Nf7,Kc4" BLACK
MOVELIST "Ng5 Kg2"
"White is forced to retreat his Bishop in order to protect his b4-pawn base.  
But now the Bishop is on a defensive diagonal, only with hardly a place to 
