OK

MOVE Kc4
"31...Kc4"
OK

DIAGRAM "a5,b4,e3,f2,Bd2,Kc2 v a6,b5,e5,e4,g4,h4,Ne2,Kc4" WHITE
MOVELIST "Be1 Ng1 Bd2 Nh3 Be1 Ng5"
"At last, Black has made inroads with his King. Now, using a coordinated 
attack of King and Knight the b4-pawn must fall.  32.Be1 Ng1 33.Bd2 Nh3 
34.Be1 Ng5"
OK

SLIDE g5 e6
SLIDE e6 c7
SLIDE c7 d5
SLIDE d5 b4
"...and White gave up. He is helpless to prevent ...Ng5-e6-c7-d5xb4. While 
the winning process was a long one, it showed the way that a Knight can 
dominate a Bishop and vice versa. A fine example."
OK

"One of the Knight's specialties is attacking in different directions. A 
Knight in the middle of the board can attack eight different squares 
simultaneously."
OK

"This gives the Knight a great potential for forking the enemy pieces."
OK

DIAGRAM "Nc7 v Ra8,Ke8" WHITE
"This example shows the most common type of Knight fork."
OK

"Black's King is in check and he can kiss his a8-Rook goodbye."
OK

"Forks in which the King, Queen, and Rook are simultaneously attacked are 
known as a 'family fork.'"
OK

DIAGRAM "Nc7 v Ra8,Qd5,Ke8" WHITE
"By adding a Queen, we get a family fork."
OK

"All three Black pieces are under attack at once. This ability to jump 
around the board and fork pieces makes the hostile Knights pieces to watch 
out for."
OK

"While Knights and Queens can't work together to form a battery, they do 
work very well together in a number of circumstances. The best known example 
is the Smothered Mate."
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Qd5,Ng5,Kh1 v g7,h7,Rd8,Rb8,Qb6,Kh8" WHITE
"White seems to be having it tough. His Queen is under 
attack and Black has a back rank mate threat."
OK

MOVELIST "Nf7 Kg8 Nh6"
"White had better come up with something in a hurry. Fortunately, White is 
spared defeat by a nifty little mate. White plays 1.Nf7+ Kg8 2.Nh6!++"
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Qd5,Nh6,Kh1 v g7,h7,Rd8,Rb8,Qb6,Kg8" BLACK
"Black is now in double check and must move his King. He can't play 2...Kf8 
as 3.Qf7# is checkmate. Black goes back to hide in his corner."
OK

MOVE Kh8
"2...Kh8"
OK

"White could now repeat the position with 3.Nf7+ and a perpetual check and a 
drawn game. But, with a gleam in his eye, White finds the mate."
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Qd5,Nh6,Kh1 v g7,h7,Rd8,Rb8,Qb6,Kh8" WHITE
MOVE Qg8
"3.Qg8+!!"
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Qg8,Nh6,Kh1 v g7,h7,Rd8,Rb8,Qb6,Kh8" BLACK
"A fine move. Black is forced to take away his King's escape square.  
3...Rxg8 4.Nf7#"
MOVELIST "Rxg8 Nf7"
OK

DIAGRAM "f2,g2,h2,Nf7,Kh1 v g7,h7,Rg8,Rb8,Qb6,Kh8" BLACK
"A beautiful smothered mate. This Knight smothered mate occurs in a lot 
of games from a number of positions. It's worth keeping in mind."
OK

DIAGRAM "b2,Ne5,Kb1,Qh1 v f7,Bh8,Kg8,Qd8" WHITE
"Challenge:  How should White continue?"
CHALLENGE Qxh8+ "Good move!"
OK

"Qxh8+"
OK

DIAGRAM "b2,Ne5,Kb1,Qh8 v f7,Kg8,Qd8" BLACK
MOVE Kxh8
"Challenge:  After Kxh8, what next?"
CHALLENGE Nxf7+ "Right!"
OK

"Nxf7+"
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
MOVE d4
MOVE Nf6 
MOVE c4
MOVE e5
MOVE dxe5
MOVE Ng4
MOVE Nf3
MOVE Nc6
MOVE Bf4
MOVE Bb4
MOVE Nbd2
MOVE Qe7
MOVE a3
MOVE Ngxe5
"In the opening, after 1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 e5 3.dxe5 Ng4 
4.Nf3 Nc6 5.Bf4 Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 Qe7 7.a3 Ngxe5.  What prevents 
White from capturing the Bishop, a3xb4?"
OK

"Nd3"
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
MOVE e4
MOVE c6
MOVE d4
MOVE d5
MOVE Nc3
MOVE dxe4
MOVE Nxe4
MOVE Nd7
MOVE Qe2
"Another opening, 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nd7 5.Qe2.  
Why can't Black develop, Ng8-f6?"
OK

"Nd6"
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

//=======================================================================
//Chapter Nine"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 8

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

"Thus far, the tutorial has focused upon recognizing the various pawn 
skeletons and relating the pawn position for formulating a plan. This has 
been done for the Rook, Bishop and Knight."
OK

"I've avoided using the pawn structure to work with the Queen for the 
following reasons:"
OK

"1)  The Queen is so powerful, she needn't necessarily follow the pawn 
structure to find a course of action. She can create her own."
OK

"2)  Because the Queen has the combined powers of Rook and Bishop, you can 
use the same guiding principles to play with the Queen as for these pieces."
OK

"3)  There are no 'general priniciples' governing the Queen's actions. If 
told that a Queen operates well in an open position, there are thousands of 
examples where she's done very well in a closed position! And vice versa."
OK

"As they say, a Queen does her own thing! Despite point three, the following 
generalities should govern a Queen's movements in most situations."
OK

"a)  The Queen is a powerful, long range piece.  She prefers an open position."
OK

"b)  The Queen is a great attacker. Her ability to move in many directions 
makes her a real threat to the opposition."
OK

"c)  The Queen is an excellent support piece. She can 'back up' a Rook or 
Bishop, easily creating a nice battery."
OK

"d)  The Queen is a rotten blockader for a passed pawn. Imagine the high 
Queen having to use her powers to keep watch over a lowly pawn.  Humiliating."
OK

"So, instead of trying to point out specific do's and don'ts regarding the 
Queen, I've saved this final chapter to review a lot of what I've said and 
to show a few different positions, looking at them from the perspectives 
taught in this tutorial."
OK

BOARD r2qr1k1/pp1nbppp/2p1p3/8/PPPP4/5N2/3BQPPP/R4RK1 WHITE
"This diagram was reached in the game Y. Seirawan v. V. Kortchnoi, Monaco 
1993,"
OK

"after 1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5 3.Nd2 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bd7 5.Nf3 Bc6 6.Bd3 Nd7 
7.Qe2 Ngf6 8.O-O Bxe4 9.Bxe4 Nxe4 10.Qxe4 c6 11.c4 Be7 12.Bd2 O-O 
13.b4 Nf6 14.Qe2 Re8 15.a4 Nd7"
OK

"This is a closed position with the battle still ahead. The main feature of 
the position is the advanced pawns on the queenside."
OK

"Pushing all your pawns on a flank is known as a pawn storm. The idea is to 
force some open files in order to penetrate the opponent's position. 
I hesitantly played"
OK 

MOVE c5
"16.c5!"
OK

"This move considerably sharpens the fight. The d5-square now becomes a 
perfect outpost for Black's Knight. But White gains his own trumps, the 
ability to force open the b-file."
OK

MOVELIST "Nf6 b5 Nd5 bxc6 bxc6 Qa6"
"16...Nf6 17.b5! Nd5 18.bxc6 bxc6 19.Qa6."
OK

BOARD r2qr1k1/p3bppp/Q1p1p3/2Pn4/P2P4/5N2/3B1PPP/R4RK1 BLACK
"It is clear that White is targeting Black's c6-pawn. This move provokes a 
Queen exchange, after which it will be more difficult for Black to prevent 
White's b-file penetration, while simultaneously defending the c-pawn."
OK

MOVELIST "Qc8 Qxc8 Rexc8 Rfb1"
"19...Qc8 20.Qxc8 Rexc8 21.Rfb1"
OK

BOARD r1r3k1/p3bppp/2p1p3/2Pn4/P2P4/5N2/3B1PPP/RR4K1 BLACK
"Grabbing the open b-file. This Rook is used, rather than the a-Rook, because 
White is preparing to march his King into the center, Kg1-f1."
OK

MOVE f6
"21...f6!"
OK
                                             
BOARD r1r3k1/p3b1pp/2p1pp2/2Pn4/P2P4/5N2/3B1PPP/RR4K1 WHITE
"An excellent defensive move. Black controls the e5-square, White's natural 
Knight outpost."
OK

MOVELIST "Rb7 Bd8 Re1 Rc7 Rxc7 Nxc7 Bf4"
"22.Rb7 Bd8 23.Re1 Rc7 24.Rxc7 Nxc7 25.Bf4!"
OK

BOARD r2b2k1/p1n3pp/2p1pp2/2P5/P2P1B2/5N2/5PPP/4R1K1 BLACK
"We know from the d4 and c5 pawns that White has a potentially bad Bishop.  
Therefore, White is quick to activate this piece."
OK

"25...Kf7?"
MOVE Kf7
OK

"Black defends his e6-pawn by this natural move."
OK

"Instead, he should have taken this opportunity to sacrifice a pawn for 
activity. Best was 25...Rb8! taking the open file:  26.Rxe6 Kf7! 27.Re1 Rb4 
with fair compensation."
OK

MOVE Rb1
"26.Rb1!"
OK

"Having lured Black's King to f7, White's control of the open b-file becomes 
decisive."
OK

MOVE Ke8
"26...Ke8"
OK

"In order to protect the c7-Knight. After 26...Nd5 27.Rb7+ Be7 28.Bd6, White 
has a powerful bind."
OK

MOVE Nd2
"27.Nd2!"
OK

"A very good move. The White Knight is denied the e5-outpost. But what about 
the d6-square?"
OK

MOVELIST "Nd5 Bd6 Ba5 Nc4 Bb4"
"27...Nd5 28.Bd6 Ba5 29.Nc4 Bb4"
OK

BOARD r3k3/p5pp/2pBpp2/2Pn4/PbNP4/8/5PPP/1R4K1 WHITE
"Black has blocked the b-file with his Bishop. But his Bishop is a little 
shaky, relying solely upon the d5-Knight for support."
OK

"If Black could just play ...a7-a5, then the b4-Bishop will have found a 
wonderful outpost while clogging-up the b-file."
OK

MOVE a5
"30.a5!"
OK

BOARD r3k3/p5pp/2pBpp2/P1Pn4/1bNP4/8/5PPP/1R4K1 BLACK
"A fine move. White prevents ...a7-a5 and prepares to undermine the b4-Bishop 
by playing Nc4-e3."
OK

MOVE Rd8
"30...Rd8"
OK

BOARD 3rk3/p5pp/2pBpp2/P1Pn4/1b1P4/4N3/5PPP/1R4K1 WHITE
"If 30...a6? 31.Nb6 wins."
OK

MOVELIST "a6 Bc3"
"31.a6 Bc3"
OK

BOARD 3rk3/p5pp/P1pBpp2/2Pn4/2NP4/2b5/5PPP/1R4K1 WHITE
"Black's b-file blockade gives way. If 31...Rd7 32.Ne3 Bc3 33.Nxd5 exd5 
34.Rb8+ Rd8 35.Rb7 Rd7 36.Bb8 wins."
OK

MOVELIST "Rb7 Bxd4 Rxa7 f5 Rb7 "
"32.Rb7 Bxd4 33.Rxa7 f5 34.Rb7 "
OK

BOARD 3rk3/1R4pp/P1pBp3/2Pn1p2/2Nb4/8/5PPP/6K1 BLACK
"...and Black gave up. An excellent demonstration of using an effective 
plan based upon the pawn skeleton."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER

//=======================================================================
//Chapter Ten"
//=======================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 9

CLRHIGH
BOARD 64 WHITE

"Our next game was considered the best game of 1993. This, before the year 
was up! It was played between the world's two highest rated players, 
Anatoly Karpov and Garry Kasparov."
OK

"This was reached after the following moves: 
1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 Bg7 4.e4 d6 5.f3 O-O 6.Be3 e5 7.Nge2 c6 8.Qd2 Nbd7 
9.Rd1?! a6 10.dxe5?! Nxe5! 11.b3 b5."
OK

BOARD r1bq1rk1/5pbp/p1pp1np1/1p2n3/2P1P3/1PN1BP2/P2QN1PP/3RKB1R WHITE
"White is committing a cardinal sin. He is ignoring his development and 
leaving his King in the center.  White's best 
is 12.Qxd6 Qxd6 13.Rxd6 bxc4."
OK

"Going into an endgame spares White a dangerous middle-game attack. His 
reason for doing this was to win the d6-pawn."
OK

MOVELIST "cxb5 axb5 Qxd6 Nfd7"
"12.cxb5? axb5 13.Qxd6 Nfd7!"
OK

BOARD r1bq1rk1/3n1pbp/2pQ2p1/1p2n3/4P3/1PN1BP2/P3N1PP/3RKB1R WHITE
"Black maximizes his piece play. The e5-Knight sits on a fine square. 
The g7-Bishop is on the long diagonal and is prepared to enter the fray. 
The a8-Rook, without moving, is active along the a-file."
OK

"White has to neutralize all of these active pieces, while trying to develop 
his own. The win of the pawn wasn't worth all of this trouble."
OK

MOVE f4
"14.f4?  A completely understandable move. White wants to dislodge the 
powerful e5-Knight. However, the text has a tactical drawback that Kasparov 
is quick to exploit."
OK

"While not ideal, best was 14.Kf2 followed by Kf2-g1, fleeing the center."
OK

MOVE b4
"14...b4!  Avoiding 14...Ng4 15.Bd4, when White is well on his way to 
neutralizing the Black forces."
OK

MOVE Nb1
"15.Nb1.  White has no choice. His two alternatives are destroyed:  
15.Qxb4 c5! 16.Bxc5 Nxc5 17.Qxc5 Nd3+ 18.Rxd3 Qxd3; 15.Na4 Rxa4! 
16.bxa4 Nxc4 17.Qd3 Nb2 18.Qc2 Nxd1 19.Qxd1 Qe7 20.Ng3 Rd8."
OK

MOVELIST "Ng4 Bd4 Bxd4 Qxd4 Rxa2"
"15...Ng4 16.Bd4 Bxd4 17.Qxd4 Rxa2"
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/2p3p1/8/1p1QPPn1/1P6/r3N1PP/1N1RKB1R WHITE
"Black crashes through on his half-open 
a-file. White's lack of development spells his downfall."
OK

MOVE h3
"18.h3"
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/2p3p1/8/1p1QPPn1/1P5P/r3N1P1/1N1RKB1R BLACK
"The capture Qxb4? Ne3! with the threat of ...Ne3-c2+ wins material."
OK

MOVELIST "c5 Qg1 Ngf6 e5 Ne4"
"18...c5! 19.Qg1 Ngf6 20.e5 Ne4.  Black continues to post this Knight 
aggressively in the center. He now threatens ...Qd8-h4+."
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/6p1/2p1P3/1p2nP2/1P5P/r3N1P1/1N1RKBQR WHITE
MOVELIST "h4 c4"
"21.h4 c4!  A beautiful move. Black's plan is to play ...Qd8-a5 and ...b4-b3+ 
with decisive effect."
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/6p1/4P3/1pp1nP1P/1P6/r3N1P1/1N1RKBQR WHITE
MOVE Nc1
"22.Nc1.  White has no defense. If 22.bxc4 Qa5 wins, while 22.Qd4 c2 
24.Rc1 Nc5 and ...Nc5-d3+."
OK

MOVE c3
"22...c3.  This sacrifice of the Rook is unnecessary. The straightforward 
22...Rb2!  23.Qd4 c3 is curtains."
OK

MOVELIST "Nxa2 c2"
"23.Nxa2 c2"
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/6p1/4P3/1p2nP1P/1P6/N1p3P1/1N1RKBQR WHITE
MOVE Qd4
"Karpov now played 24.Qd4"
OK

"His main alternative was 24.Rc1 Nxe5! 25.Rxc2 Bg4 26.Nd2 Qd3! when Black's 
piece activity proves to be overwhelming."
OK

MOVELIST "cxd1Q Kxd1"
"24...cxd1/Q+ 25.Kxd1"
OK

BOARD 2bq1rk1/3n1p1p/6p1/4P3/1p1QnP1P/1P6/N5P1/1N1K1B1R BLACK
"Or 25.Qxd1 Qb6 26.Qe2 Qd4 wins."
OK

MOVE Ndc5
"25...Ndc5!  Another excellent move. Black seizes the open file and targets 
the b3-pawn."
OK

MOVELIST "Qxd8 Rxd8+ Kc2 Nf2"
"26.Qxd8 Rxd8+ 27.Kc2 Nf2"
OK

BOARD 2br2k1/5p1p/6p1/2n1P3/1p3P1P/1P6/N1K2nP1/1N3B1R WHITE
MOVELIST "Rg1 Bf5+ Kb2 Nd1+ Ka1 Nxb3"
"White now lost on time. The finish is 28.Rg1 Bf5+ 29.Kb2 Nd1+ 30.Ka1 Nxb3 
mate."
OK

"Even in this extremely tactical battle, the pawn structure aided both 
players in determining their plans."
OK

"Good luck with yours!

GM Yasser Seirawan"
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
CLRHIGH

END CHAPTER
